* Pre-introductory is Jay's ((Brochure)).
* People who send a postcard to the LLG receive the ((Level -1 flyer)).
* For a more detailed introduction, please see ((Nick Nicholas)) and ((John Cowan))'s ''[http://lojban.org/publications/level0.html|What is Lojban?]'', which has become the ((Level 0 Booklet)) for ((The Logical Language Group)), and is available from [http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/detail/-/0966028317/qid=1062895255/sr=8-1/ref=sr_8_1/102-4826386-3031311?v=glance&s=books&n=507846|Amazon Books].
* Next there is ''[http://ptolemy.tlg.uci.edu/~opoudjis/lojbanbrochure/lessons/book1.html|Lojban For Beginners]'' (the "((lessons))"), by ((Robin Turner)) and Nick Nicholas, currently being edited by Nick Nicholas.
** Robin Turner wrote [http://www.bilkent.edu.tr/~robin/lojbancourse.html|some lessons], but they were used as the basis for the above ''Lojban For Beginners'', which is more complete.
* There is a [http://www.lojban.org/files/roadmap.html#draft-textbook|draft textbook] by ((Lojbab)), which hasn't been updated in a very long time.
* A ((phrasebook)) is being compiled.
* ((Multimedia)) and other materials which aren't purely textual (sound recordings)
* Finally, the '''ultimate''' educational resource is the canonical text ((The Complete Lojban Language)), available in paper from the ((The Logical Language Group)), or in draft form [http://lojban.org/publications/reference_grammar.html|on line].

* Not quite a learning material, but the ((Beginner's Mailing List)) is an appropriate place for beginner's to ask questions.
* Many Lojban students find the ((Lojban IRC)) channel to be very valuable for getting their questions answered quickly when there are others around on the network. One can also try practicing their Lojban with others in a reasonable approximation of conversational speed.

----

Learning Materials Wish-List

* ((introductory reader))
* ((better flashcard program))
